# Force LF in the repository for everything.
#
# Not cosmetic: `scripts/build.sh` is a bash script, and a CRLF checkout makes
# bash fail on line 3 with "invalid option name" (the `\r` lands inside
# `set -euo pipefail`). Windows' git defaults to autocrlf=true, so without this
# a clone on the author's own machine produces a build script that cannot run.
#
# `scripts/win.ps1` is fine either way (PowerShell accepts LF), and keeping it LF
# matches the bytes the ASCII guard and the release tarball were verified with.
* text=auto eol=lf
